In this course, there are 18 different classes, categorized into two groups: "C" classes and "D" classes. The "C" classes are typically the most common, while the "D" classes are less frequently used. The 18 classes are listed below, with the three most common "D" classes for a course called "C++" listed first, followed by the four other classes that are most often seen in the program, and finally the remaining four classes.

CLASS NAME CLASS NAME (Nominal) 1 C (C++) C++ 2 C (C) C++ 3 D (D) C++ 4 D (D) C++ 5 E (E) C 6 C++ (C++) C++ 7 C++ (C++) C++ 8 C (C+) C++ 9 C++ (C) C++ 10 D (D) D 11 C (C+) C++ 12 D (D+1) C 13 D (D+) C++ 14 D (D+) C++ 15 E (E) D 16 C (C+) C++ 17 C++ (C+) C++ 18 C (C+) C++

A C++ program can be designed to use either a single "D" class or a combination of "D" and other classes. The second option is preferred, as it ensures a minimum number of classes are required to compile and run successfully, preventing overloading of commonly needed classes.

Here is an example C program that uses C++ libraries and complies with the "D" class specification:

#include <ctime> void myCfunction(int i, char str) { printf("%s\n", str); } int main() { int i, n, c; // Include at least one of the following: // 1. An "D" class // 2. A combination of "C" and "D" classes // 3. A "C++" class myCclass.cfunc.C.myCfunction(0); myCfunction(1); myCfunction(0); i = 0; n = 1; c = myCclass.myCfunction; // Include at least one of the following: // 4. A "C" class // 5. A "D" class myCclass.myCfunction(0); myCfunction(1); myCfunction(0); i = 0; n = 1; c = myCclass.myCfunction; return 0; }

This example is equivalent to the following program:

