A few weeks ago, we launched an initial version of the Angular CLI, a set of tools for getting started with Angular 2. These tools allow developers to easily create Angular apps with just a few clicks, including building applications using TypeScript and setting up a production Angular app.

Today, we're releasing a beta of a new feature in the project that helps developers get started with Angular 2 in a few steps: we're releasing the ng-app CLI tool that helps you quickly create an Angular 2 app in no more than a few clicks. For example, to start a new Angular 2 app:

npm install ng-app --save
npm start the-foo.app

What's new

ng-app lets you create Angular apps by using familiar Angular CLI tools for building, testing, and publishing an app. Here's what's new in version 0.5.1:

You can now navigate and manage your project's configuration by using the configuration page.

You can now set up your app for development with a template file. Create a new template file for a new Angular 2 app in a project's root folder with the help of ng-app-template:

ng-app-template <file-name>.template.ts

You can configure the name and location of your project folder and any other options using the ng-app-config-settings command:

ng-app-config-settings settings.ng-app-name

You can then preview the app in the browser with the ng-app CLI tool:

ng-app build app --browser-window-format="fullscreen" app.html

If you already have a template file in place, you can add a src/ template file containing all of your Angular 2 code as a single file:

ng-app-template src/app.html

When developing with Angular 2, you may get an error similar to:

<p>Error: No module was found with path '/' </p>

This error is caused by the fact Angular 2 does not know how to find the src/ directory of your project. You can fix this by adding the src directory path explicitly to the configuration of your project:

ng-app-config-settings "prod": { src: "src/app/app.html" }

The tool supports other components you might find useful:

ng-content : for dynamically creating ng-content components
ng-table : for dynamically creating ng-table components
ng-repeatable : for dynamically creating ng-repeatable components
ng-model : for dynamically creating an ng-model component

In addition, the ng-app.app.ts template file contains many of the common building blocks needed to build an Angular 2 app. The following are the default directives and services that you'll find in this template file for an Angular 2 app:

app.directive( 'app', function() { return { restrict: 'E', require: {}, link: function(scope, element, attrs) {... } }; });

ng-app.app.js contains all of the code that makes all of these features work together. In this file, define the Angular 2 services that will be created for your app:

angular.module('theAppContainer').factory('App', AppService);
angular.module('theAppContainer').service('AppService', AppService);
angular.module('theAppContainer').directive('LoginCtrl', LoginCtrl);
angular.module('theAppContainer').directive('AuthCtrl', AuthCtrl);
angular.module('theAppContainer').directive('SignupCtrl', SignupCtrl);
angular.module('theAppContainer').directive('Logout', LogoutCtrl);
angular.module('theAppContainer').directive('AppCtrl', AppCtrl);
angular.module('theAppContainer').directive('LoginCtrl', LoginCtrl);
angular.module('theAppContainer').directive('AuthCtrl', AuthCtrl);
angular.module('theAppContainer').directive('Logout', LogoutCtrl);

