"The notion that Trump is not a Republican and should be replaced is a sentiment shared by many. Others argue that he doesn't speak on behalf of the people and should be stripped of his public office. In my opinion, this is a complex issue that requires a nuanced discussion.

Some experts, such as Michael McDonald, a political scientist at George Mason University, have expressed concerns about the rhetoric used by protesters. "It sounds extreme" is how he described the protests.

McDonald also noted that protesters are "on a roll." His colleague, Michael Gerhardt, a professor of political science at the University of Texas at Austin, shared a similar sentiment, stating that Trump is "the best thing to happen to politics since Joe McCarthy."

Gerhardt also emphasized the focus on Republican leaders and politicians, stating that "there's not going to be any Republican leaders that stand up to Trump." He added that Democratic leaders are also struggling to find their voice.

Other commentators, such as Robert Farley, the director of communications for the Washington College Republicans, have expressed outrage over Trump's standing ovation at the Republican National Convention. "I don't think they were just giving an award," he said. "There has got to be some form of accountability."

