C++ has evolved significantly over the years, and one of the most significant updates is the introduction of new features. In this series, we'll be exploring some of these new aspects, including the "new" features.

In the first part of this series, we'll be discussing the original C++ and then moving on to the new C++. The original C++ is widely available on the web, and we'll be covering some of the key features and updates.

New C++ features in C++

The last time we had a deep analysis of the new C++ was when we reviewed the proposed standards and the GCC 4.8 release candidate. The article has a lot of useful information, so we won't be repeating it here. However, since the feature set for GCC has changed, I've decided to dig deeper.

In this article, we'll be focusing on the new features in C++. There are a few new features that have been added, and we'll be exploring some of them in more detail.

Template functions

One of the new features in C++ is the ability to use template functions. This allows us to define functions that can be instantiated with different types. For example, we can define a function that takes a single argument of type `T`, and then use it with different types.

Here's an example of how we can use template functions:

```cpp
template <typename T> struct Foo { using T::*; };

int main() {
    int x;
    Foo<float>.Foo<double>(1, x);
    Foo<auto>(1, x);
}
```

This code defines a template function `Foo` that takes a single argument of type `T`, and then uses it with different types. The `Foo` function is then instantiated with the types `float` and `double`.

The benefits of using template functions are that they allow us to define functions that can be instantiated with different types, and they can be used to simplify code.

Discovering the Art of Organizing Data

This tutorial delves into a unique sorting technique, inspired by the "Algorithmic Sort" method from the book "Sorting Algorithm" in the "Finite Field Algorithms" section. For more information, please refer to the "About" and "References" sections.

This algorithm is straightforward, yet not optimized for efficiency. It exhibits the following characteristics:

The output is a collection of organized subsets derived from the input list.

The input list is divided into elements, resulting in sublists for each element.

When sorting, you can choose any sequence order, as long as it's not intended for integer sorting. You can also opt for descending order.

The order of elements within each subsort significantly impacts the efficiency of the sorting process. There is no inherent order that surpasses others in terms of speed and efficiency.

Here's an example implementation of the sorting algorithm:

```java
public List<Map> sortNby2(List<Map> list, int n) {
    List<Map> result = new ArrayList<>();
    for (Map.Entry e : list) {
        if (!(e instanceof Map)) continue;
        result.add(e.getKey());
    }
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        Set<Map> m = new HashSet<>();
        for (Map.Entry o : list) {
            m.add(o.getValue());
        }
        result.add(m.keySet());
    }
    return result;
}
```

When executing this code, you'll see the following output:

```java
List<Map> results = sortNby2(List.of(Map.of("a", 1, "b", 2), Map.of("c", 3, "d", 4)), 2);
```

As each set returned by our sorting algorithm supports the setKey() method, you can replace this method with any desired algorithm. Keep in mind that there are limitations to the length of output that can be sorted. If the input sequence is too large, the sorting algorithm will take exponentially longer to sort it. If the input sequence cannot be sorted due to its size, the sorting algorithm will throw an exception.

For further exploration of sorting algorithms, consider consulting "Sorting Algorithms" by Ravi Moorthy or examining the examples provided above. You can even experiment with this code yourself.

