In a previous post, I showcased a fundamental implementation of a class that stores a state and updates the state based on arbitrary events. I touched on the idea of extending an external entity, which can modify its data at any time, just like the state can change. In this post, I'll delve deeper into this concept and demonstrate how to utilize EntityFramework to create a concrete implementation of the service.

public class CounterService extends ApiService<CounterService> implements SubCommand { private Context context; // Introduce a pre-update task execution mechanism private Task executeTaskBeforeUpdate(Context context, CounterService.Counter newValue) { // Initialize the context with the current value and configure the counters to the new value, // set the counters to null for the current state as it has already been updated to the previous state // Incorporate the CounterService into the context and configure tasks to initiate once they're started, // add an Execute task to run when a condition is met and a Finish task to run when the task is complete, // define methods to return a value. this.context = context; this.counter = new Counter(); context.task.add(Execute.valueOf(newValue)); context.task.add(Finish.valueOf(newValue)); this.counter = null; return (this.context || Context.DISABLED)? null : (this.context!== Context.DEBUG); } }

Instead of implementing EntityFramework in your code, the framework provides a utility for these operations. For a CounterService, we can simply call EntityFramework.addImplementation (which utilizes the existing context and addSynchronizationMethod) to obtain the following implementation in the implementation class (using the existing context):

public class CounterEntityFramework extends EntityFramework<CounterService> { //... // Initialize the Context instance, which holds the previous state and updated state entities private Context context = new Context(); // Incorporate the Context instances (all entities in the code) into EntityFramework public class Command implements SubCommand { #... // Call the implementation class from ApiContext public CfgExecutableExecutor executor(Context context, Context ctx) { Execute.valueOf(ctx.context.counter.value); Execute.finish(); return executor(context, ctx); } public void execute(Context context, Counter counterValue) { assertEquals(counterValue, context.counter.value, "should be equal before and after"); assertEquals(ctx.entityClassName + " (" + context.counter.value + ")", context.counter.getName()); } public void finish() { ctx.context.counter.set(""); } } #... }

There's no need to utilize any other specific implementations with this helper, as our previous example will work for any type of entity that implements the EntityInterface.

To test our new implementation of the counter service, we can simply create a new instance of CounterService using the context:

val counter = new Counter("Hello");

I've tried it myself, and it still works, but I'm now unable to access "Hello" because the execution of the methods is being halted after "execute" is called. What can I do?
